  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1145 PM CDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nThursday evening reveal a highly amplified synoptic pattern remains \nin place atop the CONUS, with a formidable trough over the far \nwestern CONUS and a modest ridge downstream from the High Plains \ninto the Midwest. At the surface, southwest KS resides between broad \nhigh pressure over nearly the entire CONUS east of the Rockies, and \nincipient lee troughing over eastern MT and adjacent areas. Through \nthe remainder of the overnight period, continued deepening of the \nsurface low as the upper wave approaches will maintain elevated \nsoutherly winds, supporting Friday morning lows only in the upper \n50s/near 60. Daytime Friday, the deep upper trough out west will \nmake little progress eastward, however an embedded vorticity max \nwill eject north-northeast from the Great Basin to the northern \nplains. In response, the surface trough will deepen to ~996-mb, \nresulting in an uptick in south to south-southeast winds into the 20-\n30 mph range gusting to 40 mph. On the other hand, decreasing 500-mb \nheights will shave a few degrees off afternoon temperatures, with \nhighs in the upper 70s. Friday night, winds will again hold in the \n10-20 mph range, and lows will repeat in the upper 50s/near 60.\n\nDaytime Saturday, short range ensembles indicate the strong upper \nwave will advance to near the Four Corners region. As DPVA begins to \noverspread the central plains, scattered showers and thunderstorms \nwill develop in eastern CO and spread into western KS by late \nafternoon/early evening, marking the beginning of a multi-day \nprecipitation event as the slow moving trough provides the ideal \nsynoptic scenario for rainfall across our area. Additionally, little \nto no instability will preclude any severe weather risk. \nTemperatures will also continue to decrease with afternoon highs \nranging from the upper 60s near the KS/CO border to the mid/upper \n70s across our eastern zones.\n\nSunday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles agree \nprecipitation will become much more widespread over our area as the \nupper low moves over the central Rockies on Sunday, then ejects onto \nthe central/northern plains on Monday. Current thinking is moderate \nto heavy rainfall will favor areas along and west of a Hays-Meade \nline on Sunday before expanding to the eastern zones on Monday. Once \nall is said and done, the ECMWF ENS and GEFS mean total \nprecipitation fields suggest 1-2\" of rainfall is possible along and \nwest of the aforementioned line, and 0.25-1\" to the east. Much more \nseasonable temperatures will accompany the precipitation, with \nafternoon highs staying in the 60s to low 70s Sunday through the end \nof the business week.",
